# Line endings.
#
# Everything in this repository is built and run on Linux — the pinned compiler
# image, CI, the controller container, and the device itself. Nothing here is
# ever executed by Windows. So the worktree gets LF too, on every platform:
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the repository AND checks out LF.
#
# This file exists because the absence of it was measurable on 2026-09-12.
# With no attributes and Git for Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true`,
# `git ls-files --eol` reported `i/lf w/crlf` for every script in the tree —
# including `controller/device_payloads/revoice-debloat.sh`, whose first line
# in the worktree read `#!/system/bin/sh` followed by a CR. That is an
# interpreter no device has, and the failure presents as "not found" for a
# file that is plainly there.
#
# Production was never affected: the controller reads its payloads out of an
# image built on Linux. The exposure is a file copied to a device, compared
# byte-for-byte, or hashed FROM a Windows worktree.
#
# The general rule, which is the one worth keeping: a file that will be read on
# the device or inside a container must not carry the line endings of the
# machine that happened to check it out. Setting `core.autocrlf` per-machine is
# the wrong lever — it is invisible to everyone else and produces commits that
# change nothing but line endings.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files where a CR is not a cosmetic problem but a
# broken interpreter or a broken parse. These are listed explicitly so that the
# intent survives someone relaxing the line above.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.go            text eol=lf
*.c             text eol=lf
*.h             text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
Dockerfile*     text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf

# Pushed to the device verbatim — see controller/em_firmware.py.
controller/device_payloads/**   text eol=lf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png           binary
*.bin           binary
*.flac          binary
*.wav           binary
*.onnx          binary
*.tflite        binary
*.ttf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
